              brain on days 1, 7, 14, 21, 28 and 80 (adult), and compared with
              those of Wistar rats of the corresponding ages. At birth choline
              acetyltransferase was significantly higher in spiny mice than in
              rats but the adult levels were similar, with an overall increase
              of about 5.2- and 14-fold for the former and the latter species,
              respectively. Membrane-bound acetylcholinesterase level and
              maximal density of muscarinic receptor sites in spiny mice were
              considerably higher at birth, in contrast adult levels were
              significantly lower than in rats with a respective overall
              increase of about 1.5- and over 4.5-fold. The high degree of
              maturity attained at birth by spiny mice partially depends on the
              long gestation period. However, if we consider postconception age,
