              urine (Acomys russatus, the golden spiny mouse). A panel of
              biotinylated lectins and neoglycoproteins and the
              avidin-biotin-peroxidase complex technique were used on Bouin's
              fixed, paraffin-embedded sections. Various segments of the
              uriniferous tubule in both species showed differential affinity
              for labelled lectins and neoglycoproteins. Significant differences
              were also evident between comparable tubular segments in
              laboratory and golden spiny mouse kidneys. Whether the
              histochemical expression of sugar moieties of glycoconjugates as
              well as endolectins, thus both sides of presumed
              protein-carbohydrate interactions, may be correlated to the
              various glycoproteins which would include constituents of the
              glycocalyx and domains of a variety of transport enzymes deserves
